{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":15000,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China%E2%80%93United_States_relations#November_2023_Biden-Xi_summit"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China%E2%80%93United_States_relations#November_2023_Biden-Xi_summit:\nChina–United States relations\n\n|  |  |\n| --- | --- |\n| [Map indicating locations of China and United States](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:China_USA_Locator.svg \"Map indicating locations of China and United States\") | |\n|  | |\n|  |  |\n| [China](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China \"China\") | [United States](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States \"United States\") |\n| Diplomatic mission | |\n| [Chinese Embassy, Washington, D.C.](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Embassy_of_China,_Washington,_D.C. \"Embassy of China, Washington, D.C.\") | [United States Embassy, Beijing](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Embassy_of_the_United_States,_Beijing \"Embassy of the United States, Beijing\") |\n| Envoy | |\n| Ambassador [Xie Feng](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xie_Feng_(diplomat) \"Xie Feng (diplomat)\") | Ambassador [David Perdue](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Perdue \"David Perdue\") |\n\n[![Donald Trump and Xi Jinping stand next to each other, both wearing suits](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Donald_Trump_visit_to_China%2C_May_2026_P20260514DT-1642.jpg/250px-Donald_Trump_visit_to_China%2C_May_2026_P20260514DT-1642.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Donald_Trump_visit_to_China,_May_2026_P20260514DT-1642.jpg)\n\nPresident [Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump \"Donald Trump\") with Chinese leader [Xi Jinping](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xi_Jinping \"Xi Jinping\") during his [state visit to China in 2026](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_state_visit_by_Donald_Trump_to_China \"2026 state visit by Donald Trump to China\")\n\nThe relationship between the [People's Republic of China](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/People's_Republic_of_China \"People's Republic of China\") (PRC) and the [United States](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States \"United States\") (US) has been complex and at times tense since the [establishment of the PRC](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proclamation_of_the_People's_Republic_of_China \"Proclamation of the People's Republic of China\") on 1 October 1949 and subsequent [retreat of the government of the Republic of China to Taiwan](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kuomintang's_retreat_to_Taiwan \"Kuomintang's retreat to Taiwan\"). After the normalization of relations in the 1970s, the US–China relationship has been marked by persistent disputes including China's economic policies, the [political status of Taiwan](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political_status_of_Taiwan \"Political status of Taiwan\") and [territorial disputes in the South China Sea](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Territorial_disputes_in_the_South_China_Sea \"Territorial disputes in the South China Sea\"). Despite these tensions, the two nations have significant economic ties and are deeply interconnected, while also engaging in [strategic competition](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strategic_competition \"Strategic competition\") on the global stage. As of 2025, the US and China are the world's [largest and second-largest economies by nominal GDP](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_countries_by_GDP_(nominal) \"List of countries by GDP (nominal)\"). Collectively, they account for 44.2% of the global nominal GDP.\n\nThe proclamation of the PRC in Beijing by the [Chinese Communist Party](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_Communist_Party \"Chinese Communist Party\") (CCP) chairman [Mao Zedong](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mao_Zedong \"Mao Zedong\") created a new [central government](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Central_People's_Government_of_the_People's_Republic_of_China_(1949%E2%80%931954) \"Central People's Government of the People's Republic of China (1949–1954)\") on the [mainland](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mainland_China \"Mainland China\") in October 1949. Relations between the US and the new Chinese government quickly soured, culminating in direct conflict during the [Korean War](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_War \"Korean War\"). The US-led [United Nations intervention](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Nations_Command \"United Nations Command\") was met with [Chinese military involvement](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_in_the_Korean_War \"China in the Korean War\"), as China sent millions of soldiers to prevent a US-aligned presence on its border. For decades, the US did not formally recognize the PRC, instead maintaining diplomatic relations with the [Republic of China](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Republic_of_China \"Republic of China\") (ROC) based in Taiwan, and as such blocked the PRC's entry into the United Nations. However, shifting geopolitical dynamics, including the [Sino-Soviet split](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sino-Soviet_split \"Sino-Soviet split\"), the winding down of the [Vietnam War](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vietnam_War \"Vietnam War\"), as well as of the [Cultural Revolution](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cultural_Revolution \"Cultural Revolution\"), paved the way for US president [Richard Nixon's 1972 visit to China](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1972_visit_by_Richard_Nixon_to_China \"1972 visit by Richard Nixon to China\"), ultimately marking a sea change in US–China relations. On 1 January 1979, the US formally established diplomatic relations with the PRC and recognized it as the sole legitimate [government of China](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Government_of_China \"Government of China\"), while maintaining unofficial ties with Taiwan via the [Taiwan Relations Act](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taiwan_Relations_Act \"Taiwan Relations Act\"), which endures as a major point of contention.\n\nEvery US president since Nixon has toured China during his term in office, with the exception of [Jimmy Carter](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jimmy_Carter \"Jimmy Carter\") and [Joe Biden](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joe_Biden \"Joe Biden\"). The two countries cooperated with each other against the Soviet Union. Following China's [reform and opening up](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reform_and_opening_up \"Reform and opening up\"), the trade between the two countries significantly increased starting from the 1980s, and the US gave China the [most favored nation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Most_favoured_nation \"Most favoured nation\") designation in 2001. The [Obama administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presidency_of_Barack_Obama \"Presidency of Barack Obama\") signed a record number of bilateral agreements with China, particularly regarding climate change, though its [Pivot to Asia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_Asian_foreign_policy_of_the_Obama_administration \"East Asian foreign policy of the Obama administration\") created diplomatic friction.\n\nRelations worsened during the 2010s over concerns including China's [militarization of the South China Sea](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Territorial_disputes_in_the_South_China_Sea#Chinese_policies \"Territorial disputes in the South China Sea\") and [Chinese espionage in the United States](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_espionage_in_the_United_States \"Chinese espionage in the United States\"),[[1]](#cite_note-bbc._WhyU-1)[[2]](#cite_note-2)[[3]](#cite_note-3) leading observers to speculate a [Second Cold War](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Cold_War#Usage_in_the_context_of_China%E2%80%93United_States_tensions \"Second Cold War\") between the two powers.[[4]](#cite_note-4)[[5]](#cite_note-5) In 2018, President [Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump \"Donald Trump\") launched a [trade war with China](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China%E2%80%93United_States_trade_war \"China–United States trade war\"). The relationship further deteriorated during the [COVID-19 pandemic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COVID-19_pandemic \"COVID-19 pandemic\"). In January 2021, the US officially classified the Chinese government's treatment of the [Uyghurs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uyghurs \"Uyghurs\") in Xinjiang [as a genocide](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persecution_of_Uyghurs_in_China#Genocide \"Persecution of Uyghurs in China\").[[6]](#cite_note-Bernstein-2021-6) Tensions remained high during the [presidency of Joe Biden](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presidency_of_Joe_Biden \"Presidency of Joe Biden\") from 2021. [His foreign policy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foreign_policy_of_the_Biden_administration#China \"Foreign policy of the Biden administration\") prioritized strategic competition with China,[[7]](#cite_note-7)[[8]](#cite_note-8) imposed [export controls on semiconductors to China](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_New_Export_Controls_on_Advanced_Computing_and_Semiconductors_to_China \"United States New Export Controls on Advanced Computing and Semiconductors to China\"),[[9]](#cite_note-9) boosted regional alliances against China, and expanded support for Taiwan.[[10]](#cite_note-Lau-2021-10)[[11]](#cite_note-11) However, the administration also emphasized that the US sought \"competition, not conflict\".[[12]](#cite_note-12) The [second Trump administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_presidency_of_Donald_Trump \"Second presidency of Donald Trump\")'s relations with China has been marked by inconsistency. From 2025, the administration sharply escalated the trade war with China, [raising tariffs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tariffs_in_the_second_Trump_administration \"Tariffs in the second Trump administration\"), prior to negotiating with China on a reduction in the tariff rate. Trump administration has emphasized economic competition over ideological conflicts.\n\nThe [People's Republic of China](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China \"China\") (PRC) was founded on 1 October 1949.\n\nOn 25 June 1950, the China-aligned state of [North Korea](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Korea \"North Korea\") invaded US-aligned [South Korea](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Korea \"South Korea\").[[13]](#cite_note-Chen-1992-13) In response, the [United Nations Security Council](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Nations_Security_Council \"United Nations Security Council\") passed [Resolution 82](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Nations_Security_Council_Resolution_82 \"United Nations Security Council Resolution 82\"), which authorized military action against North Korea. Although the [Soviet Union](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soviet_Union \"Soviet Union\") had veto power, at the time [it was boycotting the UN Security Council](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soviet_boycott_of_the_United_Nations \"Soviet boycott of the United Nations\") over the UN's recognition of the [ROC](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taiwan \"Taiwan\") instead of the PRC as the representative of China.[[14]](#cite_note-14)\n\nInitially, the US government saw Chinese intervention as unlikely. The People's Republic was barely a year old and it appeared that, if China was going to engage in warfare, it would be in the [Kuomintang](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kuomintang \"Kuomintang\")-controlled Taiwan, not Korea.[[15]](#cite_note-Rawnsley_2009_285–315-15) The US was opposed to the PRC's interests in Taiwan and, within two days of North Korea invading the South, US forces were deployed to the [Taiwan Strait](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taiwan_Strait \"Taiwan Strait\").[[16]](#cite_note-16)\n\nAfter their defeat in the [Chinese Civil War](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_Civil_War \"Chinese Civil War\"), parts of the [ROC army had retreated to Burma](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kuomintang_in_Burma \"Kuomintang in Burma\").[[17]](#cite_note-:Han-17) The US supported these ROC forces with the hope that they would harass the PRC from the [southwest](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southwestern_China \"Southwestern China\"), thereby diverting Chinese resources from the Korean War.[[17]](#cite_note-:Han-17)\n\nIt seemed to the new Chinese leadership that stopping American encroachment into Asia was an important issue. In a speech to the [Politburo of the Chinese Communist Party](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Politburo_of_the_Chinese_Communist_Party \"Politburo of the Chinese Communist Party\") in August 1950, Chairman [Mao Zedong](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mao_Zedong \"Mao Zedong\") stated, \"if the American imperialists are victorious, they will become dizzy with success, and then be in a position to threaten us.\"[[18]](#cite_note-18) PRC premier and foreign minister [Zhou Enlai](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhou_Enlai \"Zhou Enlai\") echoed this sentiment in a speech in September: \"the Chinese people can never tolerate foreign invasion, nor allow the imperialist to invade our neighbour at will without response\".[[15]](#cite_note-Rawnsley_2009_285–315-15) Chinese leadership could not tolerate an American-occupied state directly on its border:[[19]](#cite_note-19) Zhou Enlai warned that China would intervene in the war on national security grounds; this warning was dismissed by US president [Harry S. Truman](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harry_S._Truman \"Harry S. Truman\").[[20]](#cite_note-20)[[21]](#cite_note-21)\n\nOn 30 September 1950, the UN offensive under the direction of the US crossed the [38th parallel](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/38th_parallel_north \"38th parallel north\") into North Korea.[[22]](#cite_note-22) [Kim Il-Sung](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kim_Il_Sung \"Kim Il Sung\") held an emergency meeting with Chinese officials, appealing for their urgent entry into the conflict.[[13]](#cite_note-Chen-1992-13) The UN authorized the reunification of Korea, meaning that the entire peninsula could fall into US control.[[23]](#cite_note-23) On 19 October 1950, Chinese forces crossed into North Korea.[[24]](#cite_note-24)\n\nIn response to the PRC's entry into the conflict, the US froze all Chinese assets in America and banned American citizens from traveling to the PRC.[[25]](#cite_note-FOOTNOTELi2024a50-25) The PRC seized all American assets and properties[[26]](#cite_note-26) and began efforts to remove American cultural influence from China, including by nationalizing cultural institutions affiliated with the US.[[27]](#cite_note-FOOTNOTELi2024a3-27)\n\nIn late October 1950, China began its intervention with the [Battle of Onjong](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Onjong \"Battle of Onjong\"). During the [Battle of the Ch'ongch'on River](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_Ch'ongch'on_River \"Battle of the Ch'ongch'on River\"), the [People's Volunteer Army](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/People's_Volunteer_Army \"People's Volunteer Army\") outflanked the [UN forces](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Nations_Forces_in_the_Korean_War \"United Nations Forces in the Korean War\"), leading to the defeat of the [US Eighth Army](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/US_Eighth_Army \"US Eighth Army\").[[28]](#cite_note-28) A ceasefire presented by the UN to the PRC shortly after Ch'ongch'on River, on 11 December it was rejected by the Chinese, who were now convinced of their ability to defeat the UN forces, and wanted to demonstrate China's military power by driving them out of Korea altogether.[[29]](#cite_note-29)[[30]](#cite_note-30) The Chinese achieved further victory at the [Third Battle \n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 15000 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
