{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_42","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":15000,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_and_the_Russo-Ukrainian_War"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_and_the_Russo-Ukrainian_War:\nIn the immediate aftermath of the [2022 Russian invasion of Ukraine](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine \"2022 Russian invasion of Ukraine\"), the [People's Republic of China](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/People's_Republic_of_China \"People's Republic of China\") stated that it respects Ukraine's sovereignty but Russia's opposition to the [enlargement of NATO](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enlargement_of_NATO \"Enlargement of NATO\") should also be addressed.[[1]](#cite_note-1) It abstained from [United Nations](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Nations \"United Nations\") votes that condemned the invasion.[[2]](#cite_note-2)[[3]](#cite_note-ualberta-3) The [Chinese government](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Government_of_China \"Government of China\") has attempted to mediate between the two countries, but its proposals have been rejected as being pro-Russia.[[4]](#cite_note-asiansurvey-4)[[5]](#cite_note-The_New_Voice_of_Ukraine-China-5)[[6]](#cite_note-:21-6) In 2025, Chinese foreign minister [Wang Yi](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wang_Yi \"Wang Yi\") was reported to have told the [European Union](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Union \"European Union\")'s top diplomat that China cannot accept Russia losing its war against Ukraine.[[7]](#cite_note-:28-7) China has consistently objected to [international sanctions against Russia](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_sanctions_during_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine \"International sanctions during the Russian invasion of Ukraine\").[[8]](#cite_note-8)[[9]](#cite_note-9) International sanctions led Russia to become heavily economically and technologically dependent on China.[[10]](#cite_note-10)[[11]](#cite_note-:152-11)\n\nDrones made by Chinese manufacturers are used by both sides in the conflict, with Russia receiving a surge of key drone components from China in 2025 along with other support.[[12]](#cite_note-:27-12)[[13]](#cite_note-13)[[a]](#cite_note-14) Chinese companies and individuals involved in the export of [dual-use technology](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dual-use_technology \"Dual-use technology\") and component parts from China to Russia have drawn sanctions from the U.S., [European Union](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Union \"European Union\"), and Ukraine and condemnation from the [G7](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G7 \"G7\") and [NATO](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NATO \"NATO\").[[14]](#cite_note-:24-15)[[15]](#cite_note-Financial_Times-16)[[16]](#cite_note-:19-17) Russians have also been reported to have been trained at military facilities in China.[[17]](#cite_note-:29-18) Chinese [state media](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/State_media \"State media\") outlets and [netizens](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Netizen \"Netizen\") have often given more weight to [Russian state views](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/State_propaganda_in_the_Russian_Federation \"State propaganda in the Russian Federation\"), sometimes reposting [disinformation](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disinformation_in_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine \"Disinformation in the Russian invasion of Ukraine\").[[18]](#cite_note-19)[[19]](#cite_note-20)[[20]](#cite_note-:3-21) In addition to capturing Chinese nationals fighting for Russia, Ukraine has sanctioned Chinese companies it accuses of helping Russia produce weapons.[[21]](#cite_note-:25-22)\n\n## Chinese government\n\nOnly after the invasion began did the [Chinese embassy in Ukraine](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Embassy_of_China,_Kyiv \"Embassy of China, Kyiv\"), on 25 February, advise its citizens to leave the country immediately. On 7 March 2022, the Chinese government stated that it had evacuated most Chinese citizens. Some of the students who had been trapped in Ukraine criticised the embassy's poor response.[[22]](#cite_note-23)\n\n[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Xi_Jinping_and_Putin_inspected_the_PLA_troops-20260520.jpg/250px-Xi_Jinping_and_Putin_inspected_the_PLA_troops-20260520.jpg)](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Xi_Jinping_and_Putin_inspected_the_PLA_troops-20260520.jpg)\n\nXi Jinping and Vladimir Putin reviewing [People's Liberation Army](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/People's_Liberation_Army \"People's Liberation Army\") troops during a welcome ceremony in Beijing, 20 May 2026\n\nOn 2 March 2022, *[The New York Times](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_New_York_Times \"The New York Times\")* published an article citing a Western intelligence report which said that the Chinese government had asked the Russian government to delay the invasion until after the [2022 Winter Olympics](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_Winter_Olympics \"2022 Winter Olympics\").[[23]](#cite_note-24) The Chinese government denied the allegations, stating that the goal of \"this kind of rhetoric is to divert attention and shift blame, which is utterly despicable\".[[24]](#cite_note-25)\n\nOn 15 March 2022, [Chinese Ambassador to the United States](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_Ambassador_to_the_United_States \"Chinese Ambassador to the United States\") [Qin Gang](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qin_Gang \"Qin Gang\") wrote an op-ed in *[The Washington Post](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Washington_Post \"The Washington Post\")* stating that \"conflict between Russia and Ukraine does no good for China\", that \"the sovereignty and territorial integrity of all countries, including Ukraine, must be respected; the legitimate security concerns of all countries must be taken seriously\", and that \"threats against Chinese entities and businesses, as uttered by some U.S. officials, are unacceptable\".[[25]](#cite_note-26)\n\nOn 29 April 2022, [Zhao Lijian](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhao_Lijian \"Zhao Lijian\"), one of the [Chinese Foreign Ministry spokespersons](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spokesperson_of_the_Ministry_of_Foreign_Affairs_of_China \"Spokesperson of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs of China\"), called [China–Russia relations](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China–Russia_relations \"China–Russia relations\") a \"new model of international relations\" that involved \"not causing confrontations or targeting other nations\", rising above \"the model of military and political alliance in the [Cold War](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cold_War \"Cold War\") era\".[[26]](#cite_note-27)\n\nQuoting the Russian parliament, *The New York Times* reported that in September 2022 [Li Zhanshu](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Li_Zhanshu \"Li Zhanshu\"), then [chairman of the Standing Committee of the National People's Congress](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chairman_of_the_Standing_Committee_of_the_National_People's_Congress \"Chairman of the Standing Committee of the National People's Congress\") and the third highest-ranking [Politburo Standing Committee member](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Politburo_Standing_Committee_of_the_Chinese_Communist_Party \"Politburo Standing Committee of the Chinese Communist Party\") of the [Chinese Communist Party](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_Communist_Party \"Chinese Communist Party\") (CCP), blamed NATO for expanding next to Russia and told a group of Russian legislators that \"we fully understand the necessity of all the measures taken by Russia\" and that it was placed in an \"impossible situation\" on the Ukrainian issue.[[27]](#cite_note-:9-28) *[The Wall Street Journal](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Wall_Street_Journal \"The Wall Street Journal\")* reported that to advance its own interests, Russia has repeatedly leaked information without Chinese knowledge or ahead of China's announcements and decisions. According to people close to China's top decision-makers, had they known about Russia's intention to leak Li's comments, his \"choice of words would have been more careful to prevent China from being seen as an accomplice to Russia\".[[28]](#cite_note-:12-29)\n\nIn November 2022, during the [2022 G20 Bali summit](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_G20_Bali_summit \"2022 G20 Bali summit\"), China objected to calling the 2022 Russian invasion of Ukraine a \"war\".[[29]](#cite_note-30) In the same month, Russia's ambassador to Beijing announced that [Xi Jinping](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xi_Jinping \"Xi Jinping\") would be visiting Moscow, reportedly before China was ready for an announcement. *The Wall Street Journal* quoted people close to the Chinese leadership saying that the \"Chinese side hadn't even made a decision yet\".[[28]](#cite_note-:12-29)\n\nAccording to U.S. intercepts of Russian intelligence from the [2022–2023 Pentagon document leaks](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022–2023_Pentagon_document_leaks \"2022–2023 Pentagon document leaks\"), Russia believed that China had approved sending \"lethal aid\" disguised as civilian items. U.S. officials said that they have not yet seen evidence, but have repeatedly warned China against doing so.[[30]](#cite_note-31)\n\nOn 31 January 2024, during a meeting between admiral [Dong Jun](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dong_Jun \"Dong Jun\") and [Sergei Shoigu](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sergei_Shoigu \"Sergei Shoigu\"), Dong stated the militaries of Russia and China should be bolstering mutual trust and expanding cooperation to \"elevate the relations between the two militaries to a higher level\".[[31]](#cite_note-32) According to the transcript of the meeting released by the Russian Defense Ministry, Dong stated that China would continue to support Russia on the '[Ukraine issue](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine \"Russian invasion of Ukraine\")', and despite pressure from the United States and the [European Union](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Union \"European Union\"), \"China will not abandon its established policies and the outside world will not interfere with normal cooperation between China and Russia\". When asked about Dong's statements at a press briefing, [Foreign Ministry of China](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foreign_Ministry_of_China \"Foreign Ministry of China\") spokesperson [Wang Wenbin](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wang_Wenbin \"Wang Wenbin\") stated that China's position remains unchanged and does not provide military aid to either side of the conflict.[[32]](#cite_note-33)[[33]](#cite_note-34) In February 2024, at the [60th Munich Security Conference](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/60th_Munich_Security_Conference \"60th Munich Security Conference\"), [Wang Yi](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wang_Yi_(politician) \"Wang Yi (politician)\") discussed peace prospects with [Ukraine's foreign minister](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minister_of_Foreign_Affairs_(Ukraine) \"Minister of Foreign Affairs (Ukraine)\") and stated that China will not \"sell lethal weapons in conflict zones\".[[34]](#cite_note-35)\n\nIn April 2024, the [Royal United Services Institute](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_United_Services_Institute \"Royal United Services Institute\") (RUSI) reported that a sanctioned Russian ship transferring weapons from North Korea to Russia was moored at a Chinese shipyard in [Zhejiang](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhejiang \"Zhejiang\").[[35]](#cite_note-36)\n\n### Meetings with foreign leaders\n\n[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Putin-Xi_press_conference_%282023%29.jpg/250px-Putin-Xi_press_conference_%282023%29.jpg)](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Putin-Xi_press_conference_(2023).jpg)\n\nPutin welcomes [Xi Jinping](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xi_Jinping \"Xi Jinping\") to Moscow, 21 March 2023\n\nOn 1 March 2022, the Ukrainian and Chinese foreign ministers, [Dmytro Kuleba](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dmytro_Kuleba \"Dmytro Kuleba\") and [Wang Yi](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wang_Yi_(politician) \"Wang Yi (politician)\"), held their first phone call since the beginning of the invasion. Chinese media reported that Wang told Kuleba that he was \"extremely concerned\" about the risk to civilians and that it was necessary to \"ease the situation as much as possible to prevent the conflict from escalating\". Kuleba was reported to have said that Ukraine \"looks forward to China playing a mediation role in achieving a ceasefire\".[[36]](#cite_note-37)\n\nOn 9 March 2022, [General Secretary of the Chinese Communist Party](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_Secretary_of_the_Chinese_Communist_Party \"General Secretary of the Chinese Communist Party\") and Chinese leader [Xi Jinping](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xi_Jinping \"Xi Jinping\") held a video meeting with French President [Emmanuel Macron](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emmanuel_Macron \"Emmanuel Macron\") and German Chancellor [Olaf Scholz](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olaf_Scholz \"Olaf Scholz\") in which he stated that China was \"pained to see the flames of war reignited in Europe\" and called for the three countries to promote peace negotiations between Russia and Ukraine.[[37]](#cite_note-38) Later that month, Xi and [American President](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_America \"President of America\") [Joe Biden](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joe_Biden \"Joe Biden\") held a two-hour long meeting over video in which the conflict in Ukraine featured significantly. The American White House told the press after the call that Biden had warned Xi of \"consequences if China provides material support to Russia\".[[38]](#cite_note-39)\n\nOn 20–22 March 2023, Xi visited Russia and [met with Vladimir Putin](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2023_visit_by_Xi_Jinping_to_Russia \"2023 visit by Xi Jinping to Russia\"), both in an official and unofficial capacity.[[39]](#cite_note-40)[[40]](#cite_note-41)\n\n[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Xi_Jinping_and_Vladimir_Putin_walking_past_the_Beijing_Capital_Garrison_Honor_Guard_Battalion_and_children_with_flags_%282024%29.jpg/250px-Xi_Jinping_and_Vladimir_Putin_walking_past_the_Beijing_Capital_Garrison_Honor_Guard_Battalion_and_children_with_flags_%282024%29.jpg)](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Xi_Jinping_and_Vladimir_Putin_walking_past_the_Beijing_Capital_Garrison_Honor_Guard_Battalion_and_children_with_flags_(2024).jpg)\n\n[Xi Jinping](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xi_Jinping \"Xi Jinping\") welcomes Putin in Beijing during Putin's [visit](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_visit_by_Vladimir_Putin_to_China \"2024 visit by Vladimir Putin to China\") to China in May 2024\n\nOn 26 April 2023, Xi made an hour-long call to Ukrainian President [Volodymyr Zelenskyy](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volodymyr_Zelenskyy \"Volodymyr Zelenskyy\").[[41]](#cite_note-china-ukraine-call-42) Zelenskyy described the call as \"long and meaningful\" and stressed \"a just and sustainable peace for Ukraine\".[[41]](#cite_note-china-ukraine-call-42)\n\nDuring Xi's visit to France in May 2024, he said to Emmanuel Macron and European Commission President [Ursula von der Leyen](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ursula_von_der_Leyen \"Ursula von der Leyen\") that Beijing did not intend to supply weapons to Moscow and that it was ready to look into the issue of dual-use materials that enabled Russia's war effort.[[42]](#cite_note-43)\n\nIn May 2024, Putin visited China and [met with Xi](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_visit_by_Vladimir_Putin_to_China \"2024 visit by Vladimir Putin to China\"). The purpose of the visit was to deepen the strategic partnership between Russia and China.[[43]](#cite_note-44)\n\nOn 23 July 2024, the Ukrainian Foreign Minister [Dmytro Kuleba](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dmytro_Kuleba \"Dmytro Kuleba\") visited China for the first ti\n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 15000 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
