{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_44","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":15000,"start_index":15000,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_and_the_Russo-Ukrainian_War"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_and_the_Russo-Ukrainian_War:\nme since the Russian invasion for talks with Chinese Foreign Minister [Wang Yi](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wang_Yi_(politician) \"Wang Yi (politician)\") on ways to achieve a diplomatic solution to the war.[[44]](#cite_note-45)[[45]](#cite_note-reuters-20240723-46)[[46]](#cite_note-ukrn-20240724-47) The *[South China Morning Post](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_China_Morning_Post \"South China Morning Post\")* and [CNN](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CNN \"CNN\") reported that in a July 2025 meeting with [Vice-President of the European Commission](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vice-President_of_the_European_Commission \"Vice-President of the European Commission\") [Kaja Kallas](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kaja_Kallas \"Kaja Kallas\"), Wang Yi stated that Beijing did not want to see Russia's loss in Ukraine.[[7]](#cite_note-:28-7)[[47]](#cite_note-48) European diplomats said Wang Yi is concerned that the United States would focus more on China once the conflict in Europe ends.[[7]](#cite_note-:28-7)[[48]](#cite_note-49)\n\n### United Nations votes\n\nOn 25 February 2022, China abstained from a [United Nations Security Council](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Nations_Security_Council \"United Nations Security Council\") vote denouncing the invasion.[[49]](#cite_note-50) On 2 March 2022, China joined 35 countries in abstaining from a [United Nations General Assembly](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Nations_General_Assembly \"United Nations General Assembly\") resolution which condemned Russia's aggression and demanded Russia's \"immediate, thorough, and unconditional\" withdrawal of troops from Ukraine.[[50]](#cite_note-:16-51) In December 2023, China voted against condemning Russia at the United Nations.[[51]](#cite_note-52)\n\n### Peace proposals\n\n[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Dmytro_Kuleba_met_with_Li_Hui_to_stop_Russian_aggression.jpg/250px-Dmytro_Kuleba_met_with_Li_Hui_to_stop_Russian_aggression.jpg)](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Dmytro_Kuleba_met_with_Li_Hui_to_stop_Russian_aggression.jpg)\n\nChinese peace envoy [Li Hui](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Li_Hui_(diplomat) \"Li Hui (diplomat)\") meets with Ukrainian foreign minister [Dmytro Kuleba](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dmytro_Kuleba \"Dmytro Kuleba\") in Kyiv (17 May 2023)\n\nOn 24 February 2023, China issued a twelve-point peace plan outline, calling for a ceasefire and peace talks.[[52]](#cite_note-53) The same day, Zelenskyy indicated he was willing to consider aspects of the proposal,[[53]](#cite_note-:11-54) while Russia's foreign ministry stated that it welcomed the Chinese proposal.[[54]](#cite_note-55) Zelenskyy stated that he planned to meet Xi Jinping because it would be useful to both countries and global security.[[53]](#cite_note-:11-54) Vladimir Putin's spokesperson [Dmitry Peskov](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dmitry_Peskov \"Dmitry Peskov\") said \"we paid a lot of attention to our Chinese friends' plan\", but new \"territorial realities could not be ignored\" as these realities became \"an internal factor\" (for Russia). Peskov then rejected the Chinese peace proposal, saying that \"for now, we don't see any of the conditions that are needed to bring this whole story towards peace\".[[55]](#cite_note-chinese-peskov-56) U.S. Secretary of State [Antony Blinken](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antony_Blinken \"Antony Blinken\") questioned China's peace proposal,[[56]](#cite_note-57) saying \"the world should not be fooled by any tactical move by Russia, supported by China or any other country, to freeze the war on its own terms\".[[57]](#cite_note-58)\n\nDuring the 2023 [Belarus-China](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belarus–China_relations \"Belarus–China relations\") summit, Belarusian President [Alexander Lukashenko](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_Lukashenko \"Alexander Lukashenko\") and Xi jointly stated \"deep concern about the development of the armed conflict in the European region and extreme interest in the soonest possible establishment of peace in Ukraine[.]\"[[58]](#cite_note-:22-59)\n\nIn June 2023, China's envoy to Ukraine [Li Hui](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Li_Hui_(diplomat) \"Li Hui (diplomat)\") urged all parties to respect territorial integrity, stop arming the battlefield, and ensure the safety of nuclear facilities.[[59]](#cite_note-zhUkrEnvoyStop-60)\n\nFollowing the 2023 [Shangri-La Dialogue](about://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shangri-La_Dialogue#2020s \"Shangri-La Dialogue\"), Ukrainian Defense Minister [Oleksii Reznikov](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oleksii_Reznikov \"Oleksii Reznikov\") said that the peace plans presented by other countries such as China were attempts to mediate on behalf of Russia and that Ukraine is willing to accept a mediator only if the Russians can be convinced to withdraw from all [Ukrainian territories](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian-occupied_territories_of_Ukraine \"Russian-occupied territories of Ukraine\").[[5]](#cite_note-The_New_Voice_of_Ukraine-China-5)[[60]](#cite_note-61)\n\nChina's 12-point peace plan was presented during a 42-nation peace discussion on 5 August 2023, at [Jeddah](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jeddah \"Jeddah\"), Saudi Arabia, and live feedback was received.[[61]](#cite_note-62)\n\nIn early May 2024, China's designated envoy on the Russian-Ukrainian conflict had visited neutral nations of the [Global South](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_North_and_Global_South \"Global North and Global South\") like [Brazil](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brazil \"Brazil\"), [Indonesia](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indonesia \"Indonesia\"), [Turkey](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turkey \"Turkey\"), [Egypt](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Egypt \"Egypt\"), [Saudi Arabia](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saudi_Arabia \"Saudi Arabia\"), the [United Arab Emirates](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Arab_Emirates \"United Arab Emirates\"), [South Africa](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Africa \"South Africa\"), and [Kazakhstan](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kazakhstan \"Kazakhstan\").[[62]](#cite_note-63)\n\nOn 23 May 2024, China presented a new six-point peace plan, but according to critics, including Zelenskyy, Russia called on China to undermine the [June 2024 Ukraine peace summit](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/June_2024_Ukraine_peace_summit \"June 2024 Ukraine peace summit\") in Switzerland.[[63]](#cite_note-:20-64)[[64]](#cite_note-Reuters-65) According to sources, Chinese diplomats told [developing countries](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_North_and_Global_South \"Global North and Global South\") that the peace summit in Switzerland would prolong the war, but did not directly ask any country to abstain from the meeting.[[64]](#cite_note-Reuters-65) The six-point proposal by China and Brazil called for an international peace conference \"held at a proper time that is recognised by both Russia and Ukraine, with equal participation of all parties as well as fair discussion of all peace plans\".[[6]](#cite_note-:21-6)\n\nIn June 2024, Former [Thai](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thailand \"Thailand\") Prime Minister [Abhisit Vejjajiva](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abhisit_Vejjajiva \"Abhisit Vejjajiva\") said that because of the fundamental lack of trust between the United States and China, and their allies, \"it's so difficult to see how either side can claim legitimacy to initiate some kind of peace summit. The other side is simply not going to accept it.\"[[65]](#cite_note-66) On 14 June 2024, China's deputy permanent representative to the United Nations, [Geng Shuang](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geng_Shuang \"Geng Shuang\"), called \"on the parties to the conflict to demonstrate political will, come together, and start peace talks as soon as possible to achieve a ceasefire and halt military actions\".[[66]](#cite_note-67)\n\nIn September 2024, Zelenskyy called a peace plan proposed by China and Brazil \"destructive\" and \"just a political statement\".[[67]](#cite_note-68)\n\n### Accusations of intelligence sharing and covert training\n\nIn July 2025, the [Security Service of Ukraine](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Security_Service_of_Ukraine \"Security Service of Ukraine\") announced it had detained two Chinese nationals suspected of spying on Ukraine's [Neptune missile](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/R-360_Neptune \"R-360 Neptune\") program. One of the Chinese nationals was a former student in Kyiv, who was arrested by counterintelligence officials after they gave him \"technical documentation\" concerning production of the missiles. They later detained the father of the former student, also a Chinese national, who they said had arrived in Ukraine to \"personally coordinate\" the work of his son and deliver the documents to the Chinese special services.[[68]](#cite_note-b689-69)[[69]](#cite_note-q340-70)[[70]](#cite_note-r476-71)\n\nIn October 2025, Ukrainian Foreign Intelligence Agency official Oleh Alexandrov said that China was providing Russia with satellite intelligence to better launch missiles into Ukraine.[[71]](#cite_note-u239-72) The same month, Ukrainian media reported that Chinese reconnaissance flights had been observed in western Ukraine over locations struck by Russian forces, suggesting possible surveillance in support of Russia. Russia denied Chinese involvement, while some analysts have said that Russia's lack of access to critical parts due to sanctions and outdated satellites makes Chinese surveillance support particularly valuable for Russia.[[72]](#cite_note-y957-73)\n\nIn May 2026, it was reported that three European intelligence agencies believed that over 200 Russians had been covertly trained in drone warfare at military facilities in Beijing and [Nanjing](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nanjing \"Nanjing\") in late 2025.[[17]](#cite_note-:29-18) The training involved at least four Chinese and Russian generals.[[73]](#cite_note-74)\n\nThe coverage of the Russian invasion of Ukraine by mainland Chinese media has raised some controversies. The [European External Action Service](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_External_Action_Service \"European External Action Service\") stated that \"Chinese state-controlled media and official social media channels have amplified selected pro-Kremlin conspiracy narratives\".[[74]](#cite_note-75) BBC and CNN believe that discussions of the topic in mainland China are led by Chinese [state media](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/State_media \"State media\") outlets, including *[Global Times](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Times \"Global Times\")*, [China Central Television](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_Central_Television \"China Central Television\") (CCTV), and *[People's Daily](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/People's_Daily \"People's Daily\")*.[[75]](#cite_note-:2-76)[[20]](#cite_note-:3-21) *[China Daily](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_Daily \"China Daily\")* blamed the United States as the driving force for the Russian invasion.[[76]](#cite_note-77) The journalistic integrity of these outlets has been called into question. As an example, it was suspected that two days before the full-scale invasion of Ukraine, Horizon News, the international relations subsection of *[The Beijing News](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Beijing_News \"The Beijing News\")*, accidentally released an internal notice publicly on its official Weibo account. The internal notice included censorship guidelines that demanded the restriction of content that is \"unfavourable to Russia\" and \"pro-West\".[[77]](#cite_note-78) On the same day, an article on the *Global Times* website referred to [Donetsk](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donetsk_Oblast \"Donetsk Oblast\") and [Luhansk](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luhansk_Oblast \"Luhansk Oblast\") as \"two nations\". The article was later retracted as mainland Chinese media began to collectively refer to the two areas as \"regions\".[[78]](#cite_note-:5-79) On the day Russia launched its military operation against Ukraine, the [Communist Youth League of China](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Communist_Youth_League_of_China \"Communist Youth League of China\") posted a rendition of the Soviet patriotic song \"[Katyusha](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Katyusha_(song) \"Katyusha (song)\")\" in Mandarin on its official [bilibili](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bilibili \"Bilibili\") account. [Initium Media](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Initium_Media \"Initium Media\") saw this action as an attempt to sensationalize the military conflict.[[79]](#cite_note-:6-80)\n\nSeveral media outlets believe that Chinese media undertook selective reporting. Some Chinese media outlets received government directives to \"keep [Weibo](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weibo \"Weibo\") posts on Ukraine favorable to Russia.\"[[80]](#cite_note-81) [Deutsche Welle](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deutsche_Welle \"Deutsche Welle\") and CNN questioned the avoidance of words such as \"invasion\" and \"attack\", and the bias towards information from Russian officials, as well as the promotion of anti-U.S. sentiments within China.[[20]](#cite_note-:3-21)[[81]](#cite_note-:0-82)[[82]](#cite_note-83) However, *The Wall Street Journal* believes that Chinese state-run media outlets were exercising restraint in their coverage of the conflict, an indication of the cautious stance taken by the Chinese government.[[83]](#cite_note-:13-84) [Radio France Internationale](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radio_France_Internationale \"Radio France Internationale\") believes that while China has not condemned Russia's invasion, it does not encourage its citizens to support Ukrainians, and has not openly supported Russia.[[84]](#cite_note-:7-85)\n\nOn 4 March, CCTV started the live broadcast of the opening ceremony of the 2022 Paralympic Winter Games, which was held in Beijing. During the broadcast, the chairman of the [International Paralympic Committee](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Paralympic_Committee \"International Paralympic Committee\") [Andrew Parsons](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andrew_Parsons_(sports_administrator) \"Andrew Parsons (sports administrator)\") mentioned the conflict in a speech made in English, harshly condemning the invasion and calling for peace. CCTV muted this segment of the speech, and did not release a complete translation.[[84]](#cite_note-:7-85)[[85]](#cite_note-86) A shot of 20 Ukrainian athletes applauding and calling for peace was also removed.[[86]](#cite_note-87) The International Paralympic Committee believed that censorship took place and demanded an explanation from CCTV.[[87]](#cite_note-88)\n\nAs of early March, journalist Lu Yuguang of state-run [Phoenix Television](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phoenix_Television \"Phoenix Television\") was the only foreign correspondent to have been embedded with frontline Russian forces.[[88]](#cite_note-89)\n\nWhen covering the [Bucha massacre](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bucha_massacre \"Bucha massacre\"), some Chinese state media promoted unsubstantiated Russian claims that it was staged, seemingly contradicting official statements from China's foreign ministry, which called the images from Bucha \"disturbing\".[[89]](#cite_note-:43-90)[[90]](#cite_note-MozurPaul-91) They reported on the massacre selectively or qualified it as an allegation made by Ukraine or the United States.[[90]](#cite_note-MozurPaul-91) On 5 April, [CCTV-4](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CCTV-4 \"CCTV-4\") relayed the Russian\n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 30000 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
