{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_46","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":12000,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Thomas_Shoal"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Thomas_Shoal:\nSecond Thomas Shoal\n\n| [Disputed atoll](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Territorial_dispute \"Territorial dispute\") | |\n| --- | --- |\n| Second Thomas Shoal | |\n| [Second Thomas Shoal is located in Spratly Islands](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Spratly_Islands_relief_location_map.svg \"Second Thomas Shoal is located in Spratly Islands\")  Second Thomas Shoal  Location within Spratly Islands | |\n| Other names | Ayungin Shoal *(Philippine English)* Bãi Cỏ Mây *(Vietnamese)* Buhanginan ng Ayungin *(Filipino)* Rén'ài Jiāo 仁爱礁/仁愛礁 *(Chinese)* |\n| Geography | |\n| Location | [South China Sea](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_China_Sea \"South China Sea\") |\n| Coordinates | [9°44′N 115°52′E﻿ / ﻿9.733°N 115.867°E](https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Second_Thomas_Shoal&params=9_44_N_115_52_E_&title=Second+Thomas+Shoal_type:isle) |\n| Archipelago | [Spratly Islands](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spratly_Islands \"Spratly Islands\") |\n| Administration | |\n| **[Philippines](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippines \"Philippines\")** | |\n| [Region](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Regions_of_the_Philippines \"Regions of the Philippines\") | [Southwestern Tagalog Region](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mimaropa \"Mimaropa\") |\n| [Province](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Provinces_of_the_Philippines \"Provinces of the Philippines\") | [Palawan](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palawan \"Palawan\") |\n| [Municipality](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Municipalities_of_the_Philippines \"Municipalities of the Philippines\") | [Kalayaan](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalayaan,_Palawan \"Kalayaan, Palawan\") |\n| Claimed by | |\n| [China](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China \"China\") | |\n| [Philippines](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippines \"Philippines\") | |\n| [Taiwan](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taiwan \"Taiwan\") | |\n| [Vietnam](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vietnam \"Vietnam\") | |\n\n**Second Thomas Shoal**, also known as **Ayungin Shoal** ([Filipino](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Filipino_language \"Filipino language\"): *Buhanginan ng Ayungin*, [lit.](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Literal_translation \"Literal translation\") 'sandbank of silver perch'), **Bãi Cỏ Mây** ([Vietnamese](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vietnamese_language \"Vietnamese language\")) and **Rén'ài Jiāo** ([Chinese](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_language \"Chinese language\"): 仁爱礁/仁愛礁),[[1]](#cite_note-1) is a submerged [reef](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reef \"Reef\") in the [Spratly Islands](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spratly_Islands \"Spratly Islands\") of the [South China Sea](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_China_Sea \"South China Sea\"), 105 nautical miles (194 km; 121 mi) west of [Palawan](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palawan \"Palawan\"), [Philippines](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippines \"Philippines\").[[2]](#cite_note-2) It is a disputed territory and claimed by multiple nations.[[3]](#cite_note-3)\n\nThe reef is occupied by a garrison of Philippine Navy personnel aboard a ship, the [BRP *Sierra Madre*](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BRP_Sierra_Madre \"BRP Sierra Madre\"), that was intentionally grounded on the reef in 1999 and has been periodically replenished since then.\n\nThe atoll is one of three named after [Thomas Gilbert](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Gilbert_(sea_captain) \"Thomas Gilbert (sea captain)\"), captain of the [*Charlotte*](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charlotte_(1784_ship) \"Charlotte (1784 ship)\"):\n\n* [First Thomas Shoal](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Thomas_Shoal \"First Thomas Shoal\") – [09°19′N 115°56′E﻿ / ﻿9.317°N 115.933°E](https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Second_Thomas_Shoal&params=09_19_N_115_56_E_), South of Second Thomas Shoal.[[4]](#cite_note-NGA93046-4)\n* Second Thomas Shoal – [09°44′N 115°52′E﻿ / ﻿9.733°N 115.867°E](https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Second_Thomas_Shoal&params=09_44_N_115_52_E_), Southeast of [Mischief Reef](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mischief_Reef \"Mischief Reef\").[[4]](#cite_note-NGA93046-4)\n* [Third Thomas Shoal](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Third_Thomas_Shoal \"Third Thomas Shoal\") – [10°54′N 115°56′E﻿ / ﻿10.900°N 115.933°E](https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Second_Thomas_Shoal&params=10_54_N_115_56_E_), Northeast of [Flat Island](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flat_Island_(Spratly) \"Flat Island (Spratly)\") – some distance North of Second Thomas Shoal.[[5]](#cite_note-NGA93045-5)\n\n## Geographical location\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Second_Thomas_Shoal&action=edit&section=2 \"Edit section: Geographical location\")]\n\nLocated south-east of [Mischief Reef](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mischief_Reef \"Mischief Reef\") ([09°55′N 115°32′E﻿ / ﻿9.917°N 115.533°E](https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Second_Thomas_Shoal&params=09_55_N_115_32_E_)), Second Thomas Shoal is near the centre of [Dangerous Ground](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dangerous_Ground_(South_China_Sea) \"Dangerous Ground (South China Sea)\") in the north-eastern part of the Spratly Islands. There are no settlements north or east of it.[[4]](#cite_note-NGA93046-4)[[5]](#cite_note-NGA93045-5) It is a tear-drop shaped [atoll](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atoll \"Atoll\"), 11 nautical miles (20 km; 13 mi) long, from north to south[[6]](#cite_note-6) and fringed with coral reefs.[[7]](#cite_note-7) The coral rim surrounds a [lagoon](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lagoon \"Lagoon\"), which has depths of up to 27 metres (89 ft) and is accessible to small boats from the east. [Drying reef patches](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coral_reef \"Coral reef\") are found east and west of the reef rim.\n\n## Geographical features\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Second_Thomas_Shoal&action=edit&section=3 \"Edit section: Geographical features\")]\n\nOn July 12, 2016, the [UNCLOS](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UNCLOS \"UNCLOS\") tribunal at the [Permanent Court of Arbitration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Permanent_Court_of_Arbitration \"Permanent Court of Arbitration\") concluded that Second Thomas Shoal is, or in its natural condition was, exposed at low tide and submerged at high tide and, accordingly, has low-tide elevations that do not generate an entitlement to a territorial sea, exclusive economic zone or continental shelf.[[8]](#cite_note-PCA20130712-8)\n\nSecond Thomas Shoal is claimed by [China](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China \"China\"), the [Philippines](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippines \"Philippines\"), [Taiwan](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taiwan \"Taiwan\") and [Vietnam](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vietnam \"Vietnam\").[[9]](#cite_note-9)\n\nThe [Philippine Navy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippine_Navy \"Philippine Navy\") maintains a presence of less than a dozen [Marine personnel](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippine_Marine_Corps \"Philippine Marine Corps\") on a 100 m (330 ft) long [Second World War](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_World_War \"Second World War\") US-built Philippine Navy landing craft, the [BRP *Sierra Madre*](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BRP_Sierra_Madre \"BRP Sierra Madre\"), which was deliberately run aground at the atoll in 1999, in response to the Chinese reclamation of [Mischief Reef](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mischief_Reef \"Mischief Reef\").[[10]](#cite_note-10)[[11]](#cite_note-11) The Philippines claims that the atoll is part of its [continental shelf](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Continental_shelf \"Continental shelf\").[[12]](#cite_note-12) Parts of the [Spratly](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spratly \"Spratly\") group of islands, where Second Thomas Shoal lies, [are claimed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spratly_Islands_dispute \"Spratly Islands dispute\") by China, [Brunei](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brunei \"Brunei\"), the Philippines, [Malaysia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malaysia \"Malaysia\") and [Vietnam](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vietnam \"Vietnam\").\n\nIn response to China's occupation of [Mischief Reef](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mischief_Reef \"Mischief Reef\") in 1994, Philippine president [Joseph Estrada](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_Estrada \"Joseph Estrada\") in 1998 decided to \"as well put up our own structures\". In May 1999, two Philippine ships—the BRP *Sierra Madre* and the BRP *Lanao del Norte*—were intentionally grounded on the shoal. According to Chinese officials' narrative, the Philippines partly complied with China's demand to remove the ships, towing away the BRP *Lanao del Norte* but leaving the BRP *Sierra Madre* on the shoal.[[13]](#cite_note-Green-etal-2017-13) Estrada promised that the latter vessel would also be towed away.[[14]](#cite_note-14)[[15]](#cite_note-15) However, the BRP *Sierra Madre* served as an informal outpost of the Philippine Navy until 2014, when the Philippines declared it a \"permanent installation\";[[13]](#cite_note-Green-etal-2017-13) in response, the Chinese government asked the Philippines to remove the grounded ship.[[16]](#cite_note-16)[[17]](#cite_note-17)\n\nPhilippine supply ships have avoided Chinese blockades in order to deliver food, water and other supplies to the BRP *Sierra Madre* garrison.[[18]](#cite_note-18) PRC coast guard vessels blocked two attempts by Philippine ships to resupply the garrison on March 9, 2014, thus supplies were [airdropped](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Airdrop \"Airdrop\") to it three days later. Another supply ship with replacement troops successfully reached the atoll on March 29, 2014, by sailing through shallow waters where the PRC vessels, having deeper [drafts](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Draft_(hull) \"Draft (hull)\"), were unable to follow.[[19]](#cite_note-19) Since then, the Philippine military has been sending relief and provisions by supply boats.[[20]](#cite_note-ABC-20)\n\nIn 2017, the Philippines [under President Rodrigo Duterte](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presidency_of_Rodrigo_Duterte \"Presidency of Rodrigo Duterte\") accepted a [gentlemen's agreement](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gentlemen's_agreement \"Gentlemen's agreement\") with China to maintain the status quo for the South China Sea while both sides tried to strengthen their relations.[[21]](#cite_note-21)[[22]](#cite_note-22) Under the status quo agreement, no construction materials would be allowed to fortify the *Sierra Madre* to avoid escalations.[[23]](#cite_note-23) In 2023, Philippine president [Bongbong Marcos](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bongbong_Marcos \"Bongbong Marcos\") said, \"I am not aware of such agreement. If there was, I rescind it as of this moment\".[[24]](#cite_note-24)[[25]](#cite_note-25) A year later, Marcos said he was \"horrified\" by revelations about the said \"gentlemen's agreement\".[[26]](#cite_note-26)\n\nIn November 2021 and [August 2023](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/August_2023_Second_Thomas_Shoal_standoff \"August 2023 Second Thomas Shoal standoff\"), [China Coast Guard](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_Coast_Guard \"China Coast Guard\") (CCG) vessels used [water cannons](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water_cannon \"Water cannon\") and blocked Philippine supply boats, preventing the boats from delivering essential supplies to the Philippine marines stationed on the *Sierra Madre*.[[27]](#cite_note-27)[[28]](#cite_note-28) On October 22, 2023, Philippine officials disclosed that Chinese vessels had rammed a Philippine Coast Guard ship and a military-run supply boat on October 17, during a replenishment mission to the *Sierra Madre*.[[29]](#cite_note-29) [Earlier in the same year](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Thomas_Shoal_laser_incident \"Second Thomas Shoal laser incident\"), a PRC coast guard ship intercepted a Philippine coast guard ship en route to the *Sierra Madre* and beamed a green laser light at the latter, which light the Philippine side alleged was \"military grade\" and caused its crew to suffer from temporary blindness. The incident, which China denied, led to the Philippines' filing of a diplomatic protest.\n\nIn April 2024, China stated it reached an agreement with the Philippines in adopting a \"new model\" ove\n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 12000 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
