{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_52","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":12000,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/August_2023_Second_Thomas_Shoal_standoff"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/August_2023_Second_Thomas_Shoal_standoff:\nFrom Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia\n\n| August 2023 Second Thomas Shoal standoff | |\n| --- | --- |\n| Part of [South China Sea disputes](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Territorial_disputes_in_the_South_China_Sea \"Territorial disputes in the South China Sea\") | |\n| *Haijing 5305* fires a water cannon at [BRP *Malabrigo*](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BRP_Malabrigo \"BRP Malabrigo\") | |\n| |  |  | | --- | --- | | Date | 5 August 2023 | | Location | Near [Second Thomas Shoal](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Thomas_Shoal \"Second Thomas Shoal\"), [Spratly Islands](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spratly_Islands \"Spratly Islands\"), [South China Sea](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_China_Sea \"South China Sea\") | | |\n| Belligerents | |\n| [Philippines](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippines \"Philippines\") | [China](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China \"China\") |\n| Units involved | |\n| [Philippine Coast Guard](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippine_Coast_Guard \"Philippine Coast Guard\")   * [BRP *Gabriela Silang* (OPV-8301)](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BRP_Gabriela_Silang \"BRP Gabriela Silang\") * [BRP *Teresa Magbanua* (MRRV-9701)](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BRP_Teresa_Magbanua \"BRP Teresa Magbanua\") * [BRP *Malabrigo*](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BRP_Malabrigo \"BRP Malabrigo\") * [BRP *Cabra*](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BRP_Cabra \"BRP Cabra\")    [Philippine Navy](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippine_Navy \"Philippine Navy\")   * *Unaizah May 1* * *Unaizah May 2* | [China Coast Guard](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_Coast_Guard \"China Coast Guard\")   * *Chuanshan* (3302) * Haijing 4203 * Haijing 5201 * \"Zhubi\" (5304) * Haijing 5305 * Haijing 5402 |\n| Strength | |\n| 4 patrol vessels 2 wooden supply boats | 6 cutters |\n\nOn August 5, 2023, a [China Coast Guard](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_Coast_Guard \"China Coast Guard\") ship blocked a [Philippine Coast Guard](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippine_Coast_Guard \"Philippine Coast Guard\") ship en route to the Philippine-occupied [Second Thomas Shoal](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Thomas_Shoal \"Second Thomas Shoal\") in the disputed [Spratly Islands](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spratly_Islands \"Spratly Islands\").\n\nThe [Second Thomas Shoal](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Thomas_Shoal \"Second Thomas Shoal\") (named *Ren'ai Jiao* (仁爱礁) by China; *Ayungin Shoal* by the Philippines) is part of the [Spratly Islands](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spratly_Islands \"Spratly Islands\") which is disputed territory claimed in full or in part by multiple countries including [China](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China \"China\") and the [Philippines](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippines \"Philippines\") in the [South China Sea](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_China_Sea \"South China Sea\"). The sea itself is claimed by China under its [nine-dash map line](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nine-dash_map \"Nine-dash map\") claim.[[1]](#cite_note-aljazeera-behind-1)\n\nSecond Thomas Shoal is occupied by the Philippines, which intentionally grounded the [Philippine Navy](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippine_Navy \"Philippine Navy\") ship [BRP *Sierra Madre*](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BRP_Sierra_Madre \"BRP Sierra Madre\") on the shoal in 1999.[[1]](#cite_note-aljazeera-behind-1)\n\n[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/China_Coast_Guard_fires_water_cannons_vs._PH_Coast_Guard_en_route_to_Second_Thomas_Shoal.webm/250px--China_Coast_Guard_fires_water_cannons_vs._PH_Coast_Guard_en_route_to_Second_Thomas_Shoal.webm.jpg)](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/54/China_Coast_Guard_fires_water_cannons_vs._PH_Coast_Guard_en_route_to_Second_Thomas_Shoal.webm)\n\nPhilippine Coast Guard (PCG) video showing a China Coast Guard ship firing its water cannon towards a PCG ship\n\nOn August 5, 2023, the [Philippine Coast Guard](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippine_Coast_Guard \"Philippine Coast Guard\") and the [Philippine Navy](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippine_Navy \"Philippine Navy\") were heading to the [Second Thomas Shoal](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Thomas_Shoal \"Second Thomas Shoal\") to conduct what it describes as a routine resupply mission for personnel aboard the [BRP *Sierra Madre*](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BRP_Sierra_Madre \"BRP Sierra Madre\") marooned at the feature. The mission involved two wooden boats of the Philippine Navy; *Unaizah May 1* (UM1) and *Unaizah May 2* escorted by the Coast Guard's [BRP *Malabrigo*](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BRP_Malabrigo \"BRP Malabrigo\") and [BRP *Cabra*](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BRP_Cabra \"BRP Cabra\") while [BRP Gabriela Silang](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BRP_Gabriela_Silang \"BRP Gabriela Silang\") and [BRP Teresa Magbanua](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BRP_Teresa_Magbanua \"BRP Teresa Magbanua\") was in standby near at the shoal. Navy purposely uses civilian-type ships for the resupply as part of [grey-zone warfare](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grey_zone_warfare \"Grey zone warfare\").[[2]](#cite_note-eyewitness-2)\n\nThe [China Coast Guard](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_Coast_Guard \"China Coast Guard\") (CCG) has impended the Philippine ships. UM1 was able to provide provisions for Filipino personnel aboard *Sierra Madre* after successfully outmaneuvering a CCG ship. UM2 was deterred by another CCG ship which fired water cannons towards it. The CCG were allegedly backed by Chinese maritime militia vessels.[[2]](#cite_note-eyewitness-2)\n\nChina claims that the Philippines is sending construction materials to reinforce *Sierra Madre*, an activity which it considers as illegal.[[3]](#cite_note-xinhua-tow-3)\n\n### Chinese order of battle\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=August_2023_Second_Thomas_Shoal_standoff&action=edit&section=3 \"Edit section: Chinese order of battle\")]\n\nSource:[[4]](#cite_note-4)\n\n* ![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/China_Coast_Guard_racing_stripe.svg/40px-China_Coast_Guard_racing_stripe.svg.png) [China Coast Guard](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_Coast_Guard \"China Coast Guard\")\n  + South Sea Bureau\n    - 3rd Bureau[[5]](#cite_note-:0-5)\n      * *Chuanshan* / 川山 (3302)[[6]](#cite_note-6)\n    - 4th Bureau[[5]](#cite_note-:0-5)\n      * Haijing 4203 / 海警 4203\n    - 5th Bureau[[5]](#cite_note-:0-5)\n      * Haijing 5201 / 海警 5201\n      * *Zhubi* / 渚碧 (5304)[*[citation needed](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed \"Wikipedia:Citation needed\")*]\n      * Haijing 5305 /海警 5305\n      * Haijing 5402 / 海警 5402\n\n### China Foreign Ministry\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=August_2023_Second_Thomas_Shoal_standoff&action=edit&section=5 \"Edit section: China Foreign Ministry\")]\n\nChinese Foreign Minister [Wang Yi](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wang_Yi_(politician) \"Wang Yi (politician)\") urged the Philippines to work with China to resolve the [South China Sea dispute](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_China_Sea_dispute \"South China Sea dispute\") in response to the Second Thomas Shoal incident. The Chinese foreign ministry has also tried to dissuade the United States from interfering in the territorial dispute.[[7]](#cite_note-7)[[8]](#cite_note-8)\n\nThe United States reaffirmed its commitment to its [alliance with the Philippines](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mutual_Defense_Treaty_(United_States–Philippines) \"Mutual Defense Treaty (United States–Philippines)\") in response to the incident. Australia, Canada, France, Germany, Japan, the United Kingdom and the European Union also issued statements condemning China.[[9]](#cite_note-us-vows-9)\n\n### Claims against Filipino diplomats\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=August_2023_Second_Thomas_Shoal_standoff&action=edit&section=8 \"Edit section: Claims against Filipino diplomats\")]\n\nThe [Department of Foreign Affairs](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Department_of_Foreign_Affairs_(Philippines) \"Department of Foreign Affairs (Philippines)\") supposedly issued a memorandum ordering the recall of [Jose Manuel Romualdez](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jose_Manuel_Romualdez \"Jose Manuel Romualdez\"), Ambassador of the Philippines to the United States due to bribery and corruption allegations from an anonymous complaint. Foreign Secretary [Enrique Manalo](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enrique_Manalo \"Enrique Manalo\") denied such order exists and Romualdez claimed that China is launching a smear campaign to damage [Philippine–United States relations](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippines–United_States_relations \"Philippines–United States relations\").[[10]](#cite_note-10)\n\n### Alleged Philippine promise\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=August_2023_Second_Thomas_Shoal_standoff&action=edit&section=9 \"Edit section: Alleged Philippine promise\")]\n\nOn August 8, 2023, China brought up an alleged explicit promise made by the Philippines to remove *Sierra Madre* from the Second Thomas Shoal.[[3]](#cite_note-xinhua-tow-3)[[11]](#cite_note-permanent-11)\n\nThe [Department of Foreign Affairs](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Department_of_Foreign_Affairs_(Philippines) \"Department of Foreign Affairs (Philippines)\") (DFA) of the Philippines insists that the grounded ship shall remain a \"permanent station\".[[11]](#cite_note-permanent-11) On August 9, President [Bongbong Marcos](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bongbong_Marcos \"Bongbong Marcos\") issued a statement that he is \"not aware of any such arrangement or agreement\" while adding that if such exist, that he rescind such agreement.[[12]](#cite_note-12)\n\nIn response to request to show proof of such agreement, China said that there is no point in doing so following the rescission.[[13]](#cite_note-13)\n\nOn August 14, 2023,[[14]](#cite_note-14) *[Manila Times](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manila_Times \"Manila Times\")* columnist [Rigoberto Tiglao](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rigoberto_Tiglao \"Rigoberto Tiglao\") claimed that the alleged pledge was indeed made albeit \"solely verbal\". Tiglao wrote that such pledge was referenced in several documents including confidential official memoranda from the DFA.[[15]](#cite_note-15)\n\nSenators [Jinggoy Estrada](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jinggoy_Estrada \"Jinggoy Estrada\") and [JV Ejercito](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JV_Ejercito \"JV Ejercito\") said that there was no such deal made during the [administration](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presidency_of_Joseph_Estrada \"Presidency of Joseph Estrada\") of their father [Joseph Estrada](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_Estrada \"Joseph Estrada\").[[16]](#cite_note-16) The former had prior recent correspondence with [Orly Mercado](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orly_Mercado \"Orly Mercado\") who was the Defense Secretary during the Estrada presidency.[[17]](#cite_note-17) He also pointed out that the promise is \"illogical\" given that President Estrada himself instructed the Armed Forces of the Philippines to ground *Sierra Madre* as a move for the Philippines to assert its claim over the feature. He challenged China to name the specific Filipino official who made the supposed pledge.[[18]](#cite_note-18)\n\nFormer President [Gloria Macapagal Arroyo](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gloria_Macapagal_Arroyo \"Gloria Macapagal Arroyo\") also categorically denied making any such promise during her administration.[[19]](#cite_note-19)\n\nThe [National Security Council](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Security_Council_(Philippines) \"National Security Council (Philippines)\") claim that China is using \"political operators\" to undermine the Philippines' position on the South China Sea dispute, naming the alleged promise as one of China's methods of \"psychological warfare\" to sway Filipino public opinion on the matter.[[20]](#cite_note-operators-20)\n\nIn April 2024, Philippine president Bongbong Marcos said he was convinced that former president [Rodrigo Duterte](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rodrigo_Duterte \"Rodrigo Duterte\") had made a secret deal with China. Duterte admitted on April 11 that he had made a \"status quo\" \"gentleman’s agreement\" not to repair or reinforce BRP *Sierra Madre*. Food and water were allowed to be brought to the sailors, but construction materials would not be permitted.[[21]](#cite_note-21)\n\nThree Filipino critics on China's territorial claims were offered compensation by unidentified individuals in July to comment on Vietnam's alleged militarization of the South China Sea. The three w\n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 12000 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
