{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_86","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":15000,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China%E2%80%93Philippines_relations"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China%E2%80%93Philippines_relations:\nChina–Philippines relations\n\n|  |  |\n| --- | --- |\n| [Map indicating locations of China and Philippines](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:China_Philippines_Locator.svg \"Map indicating locations of China and Philippines\") | |\n|  | |\n|  |  |\n| [China](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China \"China\") | [Philippines](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippines \"Philippines\") |\n| Diplomatic mission | |\n| Chinese Embassy, Makati | Philippine Embassy, Beijing |\n| Envoy | |\n| [Ambassador](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_ambassadors_of_China_to_the_Philippines \"List of ambassadors of China to the Philippines\") [Jing Quan](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jing_Quan \"Jing Quan\") | Ambassador [Jaime FlorCruz](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jaime_FlorCruz \"Jaime FlorCruz\") |\n\nDiplomatic relations between the [People's Republic of China](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/People's_Republic_of_China \"People's Republic of China\") and the [Philippines](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippines \"Philippines\") were established in June 1975. Relations peaked during the Philippine presidencies of [Gloria Macapagal Arroyo](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presidency_of_Gloria_Macapagal_Arroyo \"Presidency of Gloria Macapagal Arroyo\") and [Rodrigo Duterte](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presidency_of_Rodrigo_Duterte \"Presidency of Rodrigo Duterte\"). However, they have deteriorated due to [territorial disputes in the South China Sea](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Territorial_disputes_in_the_South_China_Sea \"Territorial disputes in the South China Sea\"), particularly since the 2012 [Scarborough Shoal standoff](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scarborough_Shoal_standoff \"Scarborough Shoal standoff\"); in 2013, the Philippine government under President [Benigno Aquino III](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benigno_Aquino_III \"Benigno Aquino III\") in 2013 [filed an arbitration case](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_China_Sea_Arbitration \"South China Sea Arbitration\") against China over China's maritime claims. The policy of current Philippine president [Bongbong Marcos](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bongbong_Marcos \"Bongbong Marcos\") aims for distancing relations between the Philippines and China in favor of the country's [relationship with the United States](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippines–United_States_relations \"Philippines–United States relations\"). The current [policy](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foreign_policy_of_Xi_Jinping \"Foreign policy of Xi Jinping\") of the [general secretary of the Chinese Communist Party](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_Secretary_of_the_Chinese_Communist_Party \"General Secretary of the Chinese Communist Party\") aims for greater influence over the Philippines, and the region in general, while combating American influence.\n\nDuring [Rodrigo Duterte](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rodrigo_Duterte \"Rodrigo Duterte\")'s presidency, the Philippines improved its relations and cooperation with China on various issues, developing a stronger and stable ties with the country, as well as a successful [Code of conduct](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Code_of_conduct \"Code of conduct\") with China and the rest of [ASEAN](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ASEAN \"ASEAN\").[[1]](#cite_note-1)[[2]](#cite_note-2) China is the Philippines' top trading partner.[[3]](#cite_note-3) However, average trust view of [Filipinos](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Filipinos \"Filipinos\") towards China is negative.[[4]](#cite_note-4) Relations deteriorated during the presidency of Bongbong Marcos due to increasing tensions over the South China sea dispute,[[5]](#cite_note-5) resulting in the Philippines' withdrawal from the [Belt and Road Initiative](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belt_and_Road_Initiative \"Belt and Road Initiative\") in 2023 and allegations of election interference in 2025.[[6]](#cite_note-:0-6)[[7]](#cite_note-:7-7)\n\n## Political relations\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=China%E2%80%93Philippines_relations&action=edit&section=1 \"Edit section: Political relations\")]\n\n[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Manuel_Quezon_visit_Shanghai.jpg/250px-Manuel_Quezon_visit_Shanghai.jpg)](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Manuel_Quezon_visit_Shanghai.jpg)\n\n[Philippine Commonwealth](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippine_Commonwealth \"Philippine Commonwealth\") President [Manuel L. Quezon](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manuel_L._Quezon \"Manuel L. Quezon\") in Shanghai, 1937\n\n[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/China_PH_bilateral_meeting.jpg/330px-China_PH_bilateral_meeting.jpg)](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:China_PH_bilateral_meeting.jpg)\n\nBilateral meeting between China and the Philippines, led by [Hu Jintao](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hu_Jintao \"Hu Jintao\") and [Benigno Aquino III](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benigno_Aquino_III \"Benigno Aquino III\"), at the [Great Hall of the People](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Hall_of_the_People \"Great Hall of the People\") in [Beijing](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beijing \"Beijing\"), 2011.\n\n### Imperial China and Precolonial Philippine States\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=China%E2%80%93Philippines_relations&action=edit&section=2 \"Edit section: Imperial China and Precolonial Philippine States\")]\n\nBefore [Spain](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spanish_Empire \"Spanish Empire\") colonized the [Philippines](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippines \"Philippines\"), [Imperial China](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imperial_China \"Imperial China\") acknowledged the existence of several Precolonial Philippine kingdoms and the Chinese Emperor received embassies from [Filipino Datus, Rajahs, and Sultans](about://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_the_Philippines_(900%E2%80%931565)#Emergence_of_Independent_polities \"History of the Philippines (900–1565)\").[[8]](#cite_note-8)\n\n### Spanish colonial period\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=China%E2%80%93Philippines_relations&action=edit&section=3 \"Edit section: Spanish colonial period\")]\n\nDuring the [Spanish colonial period](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_the_Philippines_(1565–1898) \"History of the Philippines (1565–1898)\"), several notable armed confrontations occurred between the authorities and overseas Chinese in the Philippines such as the [1603 Sangley Rebellion](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sangley_Rebellion_(1603) \"Sangley Rebellion (1603)\") and the [1639 Sangley Rebellion](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sangley_Rebellion_(1639) \"Sangley Rebellion (1639)\").[[9]](#cite_note-9)\n\n### American occupation (1899 – 1946)\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=China%E2%80%93Philippines_relations&action=edit&section=4 \"Edit section: American occupation (1899 – 1946)\")]\n\nDuring the [Philippine-American War](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippine-American_War \"Philippine-American War\"), Chinese nationalist figures such as [Sun Yat-sen](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun_Yat-sen \"Sun Yat-sen\") expressed support for the Philippine revolutionaries under [Emilio Aguinaldo](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emilio_Aguinaldo \"Emilio Aguinaldo\"). In 1899, Sun arranged a stealthy shipment of Japanese arms to the Philippines.[[10]](#cite_note-10)\n\nBetween 1921 and 1934, relations between China and the Philippines were marked by cooperation.[[11]](#cite_note-11) In 1927, Philippine Senate President [Manuel L. Quezon](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manuel_L._Quezon \"Manuel L. Quezon\") urged the Filipinos to distance themselves from Chinese nationalists due to fears of offending the [United States](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States \"United States\"). He believed that having links with China would hinder the Philippines' independence campaign.[[12]](#cite_note-12) By the 1930s, there were debates in the US government on granting the Philippines its independence. [Newton W. Gilbert](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newton_W._Gilbert \"Newton W. Gilbert\"), who formerly served as the Philippine Vice [Governor-General](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Governor-General_of_the_Philippines \"Governor-General of the Philippines\"), sent a letter to the [US Senate](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Senate \"United States Senate\") stating the importance of keeping the Philippines while also noting the consequences of a possible Chinese invasion.[[13]](#cite_note-NYT1930-13)\n\nAfter the Philippines became independent in 1946, it established diplomatic relations with the [Nationalist government](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nationalist_government \"Nationalist government\") of China and continued on after it lost the mainland to the [Chinese Communist Party](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_Communist_Party \"Chinese Communist Party\") (CCP) in the [Chinese Civil War](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_Civil_War \"Chinese Civil War\") that forced the [Republic of China](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Republic_of_China \"Republic of China\") to [retreat](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Retreat_of_the_government_of_the_Republic_of_China_to_Taiwan \"Retreat of the government of the Republic of China to Taiwan\") to the [island of Taiwan](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geography_of_Taiwan \"Geography of Taiwan\") in 1949, formerly a [Japanese colony](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taiwan_under_Japanese_rule \"Taiwan under Japanese rule\") that the ROC received in 1945. During the [Cold War](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cold_War \"Cold War\"), the two countries were part of the [anti-communist camp](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_Bloc \"Western Bloc\") that view the Chinese Communists as a security threat.[[14]](#cite_note-14) During the [Korean War](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_War \"Korean War\"), the [Philippine Expeditionary Forces to Korea](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippine_Expeditionary_Forces_to_Korea \"Philippine Expeditionary Forces to Korea\") under the [United Nations Command](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Nations_Command \"United Nations Command\") fought the PRC's [People's Volunteer Army](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/People's_Volunteer_Army \"People's Volunteer Army\") in multiple battles.[[15]](#cite_note-15)\n\n### Establishment of official diplomatic relations\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=China%E2%80%93Philippines_relations&action=edit&section=6 \"Edit section: Establishment of official diplomatic relations\")]\n\nIt began considering normalizing relations with the People's Republic at the start of the 1970s and the Philippines recognized the PRC on 9 June 1975, with the signing of the Joint Communiqué by leaders of the two countries.[[16]](#cite_note-Banlaoi-2007-16)\n\nIn 2009, the Philippines and China signed the *Joint Action Plan for Strategic Cooperation*, a five-year [agreement to increase cooperation](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diplomatic_partnerships_of_China \"Diplomatic partnerships of China\") in all areas.[[17]](#cite_note-Loh-17): 158\n\nIn 2016, Philippine president [Rodrigo Duterte](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rodrigo_Duterte \"Rodrigo Duterte\") and Chinese leader [Xi Jinping](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xi_Jinping \"Xi Jinping\") created the biannual Bilateral Consultation Mechanism on the South China Sea, a process allowing the two nations to peacefully manage disputes and strengthen their relations.[[18]](#cite_note-18)[[19]](#cite_note-19)\n\nOn 16 November 2017, the Philippines and China signed fourteen deals on a variety of issues, including transportation and military aid, worth approximately US$21.6 million.[[17]](#cite_note-Loh-17): 169\n\nIn July 2019, UN ambassadors of 37 countries, including Philippines, signed a joint letter to the [United Nations Human Rights Council](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Nations_Human_Rights_Council \"United Nations Human Rights Council\") defending China's policies of [persecution of Uyghurs](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persecution_of_Uyghurs_in_China \"Persecution of Uyghurs in China\") in [Xinjiang](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xinjiang \"Xinjiang\").[[20]](#cite_note-20) The Philippines was one of 16 countries that defended China's policies in Xinjiang in 2019 but did not do so in 2020.[[21]](#cite_note-axio_Mapp-21)\n\n### Belt and Road Initiative\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=China%E2%80%93Philippines_relations&action=edit&section=8 \"Edit section: Belt and Road Initiative\")]\n\nDuring the respective visits of Philippine President Rodrigo Duterte to the People's Republic of China and the Chinese Communist Party general secretary Xi Jinping's visit to the Philippines, the two agreed to a significant increase of Official Development Assistance (ODA) from the People's Republic of China as part of Xi's [Belt and Road Initiative](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belt_and_Road_Initiative \"Belt and Road Initiative\").[[22]](#cite_note-DevexODA-22) However, concerns were soon raised over the terms and conditions of the ODA funding and the lack of transparency into the details.[[23]](#cite_note-AsiaTimesODA-23) As of 2018, the delivery of that aid had also stalled, with few firm commitments put in place by the Xi administration.[[22]](#cite_note-DevexODA-22)[[23]](#cite_note-AsiaTimesODA-23) Despite this, the Duterte administration continued to make the relationship a major part of its economic agenda.[[23]](#cite_note-AsiaTimesODA-23) During the [Marcos Jr. administration](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presidency_of_Bongbong_Marcos \"Presidency of Bongbong Marcos\") in 2023, the Philippines withdrew from the [Belt and Road initiative](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belt_and_Road_initiative \"Belt and Road initiative\").[[6]](#cite_note-:0-6)\n\nSince [Song dynasty](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Song_dynasty \"Song dynasty\") times in China and [precolonial times](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_the_Philippines_(900–1565) \"History of the Philippines (900–1565)\") in the Philippines, evidence of trade contact can already be observed in the [Chinese ceramics](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_ceramics \"Chinese ceramics\") found in archaeological sites, like in [Santa Ana](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Santa_Ana,_Manila \"Santa Ana, Manila\"), [Manila](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manila \"Manila\").[[24]](#cite_note-24) During [Ming](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ming_dynasty \"Ming dynasty\") and [Qing dynasty](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qing_dynasty \"Qing dynasty\") times in China and [Spanish colonial era](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_the_Philippines_(1565–1898) \"History of the Philippines (1565–1898)\") in the Philippines, the Philippines through Manila has had centuries-long trade contacts with cities such as [Quanzhou](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quanzhou \"Quanzhou\"), [Zhangzhou](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhangzhou \"Zhangzhou\"), [Xiamen](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xiamen \"Xiamen\") in [Fujian province](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fujian \"Fujian\") and [Guangzhou](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guangzhou \"Guangzhou\") and [Macau](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Macau \"Macau\") in [Guangdong province](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guangdong \"Guangdong\"), especially as part of the [Maritime Silk Road trade](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maritime_Silk_Road \"Maritime Silk Road\"), then connected with the [Manila-Acapulco Galleon Trade](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manila_galleon \"Manila galleon\") that ensured the export of Chinese trade goods, such as chinaware, across [Spanish America](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hispanic_America \"Hispanic America\") and [Europe](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Europe \"Europe\") in the Spanish colonial empire and the constant supply of Spanish silver into the [economy of C\n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 15000 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
