{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_91","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":12000,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Li_Hui_(diplomat)"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Li_Hui_(diplomat):\nFrom Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia\n\nFor other people named Li Hui, see [Li Hui](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Li_Hui_(disambiguation) \"Li Hui (disambiguation)\").\n\n\n\n| Li Hui | |\n| --- | --- |\n| 李辉 | |\n| Li Hui in December 2018. | |\n|  | |\n| China's Special Representative on Eurasian Affairs | |\n| **[Incumbent](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Incumbent \"Incumbent\")** | |\n| **Assumed office**  September 2019 | |\n| Preceded by | *Office established* |\n| [Chinese Ambassador to Russia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_ambassadors_of_China_to_Russia \"List of ambassadors of China to Russia\") | |\n| **In office** August 2009 – August 2019 | |\n| Preceded by | [Liu Guchang](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liu_Guchang \"Liu Guchang\") |\n| Succeeded by | [Zhang Hanhui](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhang_Hanhui \"Zhang Hanhui\") |\n| [Chinese Ambassador to Kazakhstan](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_ambassadors_of_China_to_Kazakhstan \"List of ambassadors of China to Kazakhstan\") | |\n| **In office** August 1997 – March 2000 | |\n| Preceded by | [Chen Di](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chen_Di_(diplomat) \"Chen Di (diplomat)\") |\n| Succeeded by | [Yao Peisheng](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yao_Peisheng \"Yao Peisheng\") |\n| Personal details | |\n| Born | February 1953 (age 73)[[1]](#cite_note-pd-1)  [Heilongjiang](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heilongjiang \"Heilongjiang\"), China |\n| [Party](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political_party \"Political party\") | [Chinese Communist Party](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_Communist_Party \"Chinese Communist Party\") |\n| Children | 1 (daughter) |\n| [Alma mater](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alma_mater \"Alma mater\") | [Beijing Foreign Studies University](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beijing_Foreign_Studies_University \"Beijing Foreign Studies University\") |\n|  | |\n| Chinese name | |\n| [Simplified Chinese](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simplified_Chinese_characters \"Simplified Chinese characters\") | [李](https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E6%9D%8E \"wikt:李\")[辉](https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E8%BE%89 \"wikt:辉\") |\n| [Traditional Chinese](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Traditional_Chinese_characters \"Traditional Chinese characters\") | [李](https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E6%9D%8E \"wikt:李\")[輝](https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E8%BC%9D \"wikt:輝\") |\n| | Transcriptions | | | --- | --- | | [Standard Mandarin](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Standard_Chinese \"Standard Chinese\") | | | [Hanyu Pinyin](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hanyu_Pinyin \"Hanyu Pinyin\") | Lǐ Huī | | |\n|  | |\n\n**Li Hui** ([Chinese](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_language \"Chinese language\"): 李辉, born February 1953)[[1]](#cite_note-pd-1) is a Chinese [diplomat](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diplomat \"Diplomat\") who serves as the [Chinese Special Representative for Eurasian Affairs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_Special_Representative_for_Eurasian_Affairs?action=edit&redlink=1 \"Chinese Special Representative for Eurasian Affairs (page does not exist)\") [[zh](https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E4%B8%AD%E5%9B%BD%E6%94%BF%E5%BA%9C%E6%AC%A7%E4%BA%9A%E4%BA%8B%E5%8A%A1%E7%89%B9%E5%88%AB%E4%BB%A3%E8%A1%A8%E5%88%97%E8%A1%A8 \"zh:中国政府欧亚事务特别代表列表\")] since August 2019. He previously served as the [Chinese Ambassador to Russia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_ambassadors_of_China_to_Russia \"List of ambassadors of China to Russia\") (2009–2019) and [Kazakhstan](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_ambassadors_of_China_to_Kazakhstan \"List of ambassadors of China to Kazakhstan\") (1997–2000), and Vice Minister of Foreign Affairs (2008–2009).[[2]](#cite_note-:2-2)\n\nAfter graduation from the [Beijing Foreign Studies University](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beijing_Foreign_Studies_University \"Beijing Foreign Studies University\") in 1975, Li joined the [Ministry of Foreign Affairs of China](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ministry_of_Foreign_Affairs_of_China \"Ministry of Foreign Affairs of China\"). From 1975 to 1981, he worked in the [USSR](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USSR \"USSR\") and Europe Department of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and from 1981 to 1985, he worked as an [Attaché](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attach%C3%A9 \"Attaché\"), [Second Secretary](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Secretary \"Second Secretary\") and Third Secretary at the [Chinese Embassy in the USSR](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Embassy_of_China,_Moscow \"Embassy of China, Moscow\").[[2]](#cite_note-:2-2)\n\nAfter his return to China in 1985, he served as the Second Secretary, Deputy Head of the Department and First Secretary in the USSR and Europe\nDepartment of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs. From 1991 to 1992, he was the first secretary of the Chinese Embassy in the USSR and following the [dissolution of the Soviet Union](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dissolution_of_the_Soviet_Union \"Dissolution of the Soviet Union\") in 1991, became the first secretary of the Chinese Embassy in [Russia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russia \"Russia\").[[2]](#cite_note-:2-2)\n\nIn 1992, he was appointed as the First Secretary and adviser at the Chinese Embassy in Kazakhstan. From 1995 to 1997, he held the positions of Counsellor and Deputy Director General of the Eurasian Department at the Ministry of Foreign Affairs.[[3]](#cite_note-s339-3) In 1997, he was appointed as [Chinese Ambassador to Kazakhstan](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_ambassadors_of_China_to_Kazakhstan \"List of ambassadors of China to Kazakhstan\") and served this position till 1999.[[2]](#cite_note-:2-2)\n\nThroughout Li Hui's ambassadorship to Kazakhstan, he vigorously advocated for [China-Kazakhstan border](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China-Kazakhstan_border \"China-Kazakhstan border\") negotiations, energy collaboration, and bilateral economic and trade interactions, establishing a robust foundation for the enduring stability of China-Kazakhstan relations. To delineate certain small sections of the border more precisely, additional agreements were signed on 24 September 1997 and 4 July 1998.[[4]](#cite_note-law469-4)[[5]](#cite_note-mfa.gov.kz-5)\n\n[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Dmitry_Medvedev_with_Li_Hui.jpg/250px-Dmitry_Medvedev_with_Li_Hui.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Dmitry_Medvedev_with_Li_Hui.jpg)\n\nLi Hui presented his credentials to [Russian President](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_Russia \"President of Russia\") [Dmitry Medvedev](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dmitry_Medvedev \"Dmitry Medvedev\") on 16 December 2009\n\nFrom 1999 to 2003, he served as the head of the Europe and Asia Department of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and from 2003 to 2008, he served\nassistant to the [Minister of Foreign Affairs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minister_of_Foreign_Affairs_of_the_People's_Republic_of_China \"Minister of Foreign Affairs of the People's Republic of China\").[[2]](#cite_note-:2-2)\n\nIn 2008, he was appointed as Vice Minister of Foreign Affairs. On 13 August 2009, he was appointed as the Chinese Ambassador to Russia by the then [11th National People's Congress](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/11th_National_People's_Congress \"11th National People's Congress\"), succeeding [Liu Guchang](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liu_Guchang \"Liu Guchang\"). He presented his credentials to [Russian President](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_Russia \"President of Russia\") [Dmitry Medvedev](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dmitry_Medvedev \"Dmitry Medvedev\") on 16 December 2009.[[6]](#cite_note-16dec09-6)\n\nLi served this position till 10 August 2019 and was the longest serving Chinese Ambassador to Russia.[[1]](#cite_note-pd-1)[[2]](#cite_note-:2-2)[[7]](#cite_note-7) Throughout Li Hui's service as China's ambassador to Russia, the leaders of China and Russia—[Chinese leaders](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paramount_leader \"Paramount leader\") [Hu Jintao](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hu_Jintao \"Hu Jintao\") (until 2012) and [Xi Jinping](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xi_Jinping \"Xi Jinping\") (since 2012), alongside Russian Presidents [Dmitry Medvedev](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dmitry_Medvedev \"Dmitry Medvedev\") (2008–2012) and [Vladimir Putin](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vladimir_Putin \"Vladimir Putin\") (2012-present)—have conducted numerous high-level meetings. During 2013–2014, the parties advocated for the execution of a Sino-Russian gas supply agreement, valued at $400 billion, pertaining to the [Power of Siberia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Power_of_Siberia \"Power of Siberia\") gas pipeline.[[8]](#cite_note-v290-8)[[9]](#cite_note-n460-9)\n\n### Special Representative for European Affairs\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Li_Hui_(diplomat)&action=edit&section=5 \"Edit section: Special Representative for European Affairs\")]\n\n[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Dmytro_Kuleba_met_with_Li_Hui_to_stop_Russian_aggression.jpg/250px-Dmytro_Kuleba_met_with_Li_Hui_to_stop_Russian_aggression.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Dmytro_Kuleba_met_with_Li_Hui_to_stop_Russian_aggression.jpg)\n\nLi Hui meets with Ukrainian Foreign Minister [Dmytro Kuleba](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dmytro_Kuleba \"Dmytro Kuleba\") in Kyiv, 17 May 2023\n\nIn August 2019, he was appointed as the Chinese Special Representative for Eurasian Affairs. In a 2020 essay for the [Chinese People's Institute of Foreign Affairs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_People's_Institute_of_Foreign_Affairs \"Chinese People's Institute of Foreign Affairs\"), he wrote that China and Russia \"will, as always, show firm support for each other’s efforts to uphold one’s own sovereignty, security, territorial integrity and other core interests.\"[[2]](#cite_note-:2-2)[[10]](#cite_note-10)\n\nOn 26 April 2023, he was appointed as Chinese special representative for the settlement of the [Russo-Ukrainian War](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_War \"Russo-Ukrainian War\") and visited Russia, Ukraine, [Germany](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Germany \"Germany\"), [Poland](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poland \"Poland\") and [France](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/France \"France\") to take part in the talks seeking to resolve the conflict. From 16 to 17 May 2023, Li visited Ukraine and during the visit, he met with the Ukrainian Foreign Minister [Dmytro Kuleba](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dmytro_Kuleba \"Dmytro Kuleba\"). The parties discussed the principles of restoring a stable and just peace based on respect for the sovereignty and territorial integrity of Ukraine, while Kuleba stated that Ukraine would not accept any proposals that would involve the loss of its territories or freezing of the conflict. Li also took part in the briefing of current security situation in Ukraine at the office of the [President of Ukraine](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_Ukraine \"President of Ukraine\").[[11]](#cite_note-11)[[12]](#cite_note-12)[[13]](#cite_note-13) European officials reportedly criticized [China's peace plan](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China's_peace_plan \"China's peace plan\") as an attempt at \"freezing\" the conflict in place and splitting the West in pushing Ukraine cease-fire,[[14]](#cite_note-14) though this view of China's attempt was disputed by Kuleba.[[15]](#cite_note-Kuleba-15)\n\n[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Li_Hui%2C_W%C5%82adys%C5%82aw_Teofil_Bartoszewski_2.jpg/250px-Li_Hui%2C_W%C5%82adys%C5%82aw_Teofil_Bartoszewski_2.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Li_Hui,_W%C5%82adys%C5%82aw_Teofil_Bartoszewski_2.jpg)\n\nPolish Deputy Minister [Władysław Teofil Bartoszewski](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/W%C5%82adys%C5%82aw_Bartoszewski \"Władysław Bartoszewski\") met Li Hui on March 6, 2024\n\nIn March 2024, during his journey to Russia, Ukraine, and pertinent European nations, Li Hui engaged in his second round of comprehensive discussions with the parties over the implementation of a ceasefire, the prompt conclusion of the conflict, and the advancement of a political resolution to the problem.[[16]](#cite_note-q176-16) From May 3 to 9\n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 12000 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
